
HashSet Data Structure - Full Beginner Guide
(English + Hinglish Explanation)

What is a HashSet?
A HashSet is a data structure in Java that stores unique elements and provides fast access.
(Yeh ek data structure hai jo sirf unique elements store karta hai aur fast access provide karta hai.)

It is part of Java’s java.util package.
(Ye Java ke java.util package me hota hai.)

Why Use HashSet?
Unique Elements Only – It does not allow duplicates.
(Duplicate values store nahi hoti hain, sirf unique elements hote hain.)
Fast Operations – Average case O(1) time complexity for insertion, deletion, and search.
(Insertion, deletion aur search fast hota hai, kyunki average case me O(1) time lagta hai.)
No Indexing – It does not maintain any order of elements.
(Isme elements kisi particular order me store nahi hote.)
Efficient for Lookups – Useful when checking if an element exists in a set.
(Agar kisi element ko fast check karna ho ki exist karta hai ya nahi, toh yeh useful hai.)
How HashSet Works Internally?
HashSet uses a HashMap internally.
(Iske andar ek HashMap hota hai jo data ko store karta hai.)
When you add an element, it is actually added as a key in the HashMap.
(Jab aap koi element add karte ho, toh woh HashMap ki key ke roop me store hota hai.)
No duplicate keys are allowed in HashMap, so HashSet automatically prevents duplicates.
(HashMap me duplicate keys allowed nahi hoti, is wajah se HashSet me bhi duplicate elements nahi hote.)
Basic Operations in HashSet
1. Creating a HashSet
java
Copy
Edit
import java.util.HashSet;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating a HashSet
        HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

        // Adding elements
        set.add(10);
        set.add(20);
        set.add(30);
        set.add(10); // Duplicate element

        System.out.println(set);
    }
}
Output:

plaintext
Copy
Edit
[10, 20, 30]
Explanation:

add(value) method se values insert hoti hain.
Duplicate value 10 ignore ho gayi, kyunki HashSet duplicates allow nahi karta.
2. Checking If an Element Exists
java
Copy
Edit
System.out.println(set.contains(20)); // Output: true
System.out.println(set.contains(40)); // Output: false
Explanation:

contains(value) check karta hai ki koi value exist karti hai ya nahi.
3. Removing an Element
java
Copy
Edit
set.remove(20);
System.out.println(set); // Output: [10, 30]
Explanation:

remove(value) se element delete hota hai.
4. Iterating Over a HashSet
java
Copy
Edit
for (int num : set) {
    System.out.println(num);
}
